==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|wynsum||winsome, pleasant, joyful, merry}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*wunisamaz||joyful}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*wunjō||joy, delight, desire}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*wun-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*wenh₁-||to wish, love}}, equivalent to {{suffix|winne|some|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|winsome}}, {{m|sco|wunsome||charming, comely, pleasing}}, {{cog|gmh|wunnesam||winsome, joyful, delightful}}, {{cog|ang|wynn||joy, rapture, pleasure, delight}}, {{cog|de|Wonne||bliss, delight, joy}}. More at {{l|en|winne}}, {{l|en|winly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈwɪn.səm/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# [[charming|Charming]], winning, engaging; inspiring [[trust]] and [[approval]], especially if in an innocent manner.
#: ''His bedside manner was especially '''winsome'''.''
#* '''1922''', {{w|James Joyce}}, ''[[w:Ulysses (novel)|Ulysses]]'' Chapter 13
#*: Gerty MacDowell who was seated near her companions, lost in thought, gazing far away into the distance was, in very truth, as fair a specimen of '''winsome''' Irish girlhood as one could wish to see.

